{"abstain":false,"answer":"Yes. You can purchase shares directly from a corporation, for example during its Initial Public Offering (IPO), although such offerings are infrequent. [S1]

Many companies also offer a Direct Stock Plan (DSP) or Dividend Reinvestment Plan (DRIP), allowing direct purchases at little or no transaction cost, sometimes through a third-party transfer agent. The shares are issued in your name. [S2]

With a DSP, you can begin buying without already being a shareholder. For the DRIP arrangement described in the document, you must first purchase a share outside the plan and register it in your name; then you can enroll and purchase additional shares directly. [S3]","citations":["S1","S2","S3"]}
